To create an HTML navigation bar dynamically inside , you can use JavaScript to generate the necessary HTML structure and inject it into the DOM. Here's how you can do it step by step:

---

### **1. Define the Navigation Items**
Create an array of objects to represent the navigation items. Each object should include properties like the link URL, display name, and an optional icon.

```javascript
const NAV_ITEMS = [
    { name: "Dashboard", url: "dashboard.html", icon: "dashboard-icon" },
    { name: "Audit Log", url: "audit-log.html", icon: "audit-icon" },
    { name: "Settings", url: "settings.html", icon: "settings-icon" },
];
```

---

### **2. Create a Function to Generate the HTML**
Write a function that loops through the `NAV_ITEMS` array and generates the HTML for the navigation bar.

```javascript
function renderNavigationMenu() {
    const navBar = document.getElementById("navigation-bar"); // Target the nav container
    let navHTML = `
        <ul class="nav-menu">
            ${NAV_ITEMS.map(item => `
                <li class="nav-item">
                    <a href="${item.url}" class="nav-link">
                        <span class="nav-icon">${item.icon}</span>
                        <span class="nav-text">${item.name}</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            `).join("")}
        </ul>
    `;
    navBar.innerHTML = navHTML; // Inject the generated HTML into the nav container
}
```

---

### **3. Add Event Listeners for Dynamic Behavior**
If you need to handle events like highlighting the active page or toggling the nav bar, you can add event listeners.

```javascript
function setActiveNavItem() {
    const currentPage = window.location.pathname.split("/").pop();
    const navLinks = document.querySelectorAll(".nav-link");

    navLinks.forEach(link => {
        if (link.getAttribute("href") === currentPage) {
            link.classList.add("active");
        } else {
            link.classList.remove("active");
        }
    });
}
```

---

### **4. Initialize the Navigation Bar**
Call the  function and any other setup functions when the DOM is fully loaded.

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
    renderNavigationMenu();
    setActiveNavItem();
});
```

---

### **5. Example HTML Structure**
Ensure your HTML file has a placeholder for the navigation bar:

```html
<nav id="navigation-bar">
    <!-- Navigation content will be dynamically injected here -->
</nav>
```

---

### **6. Optional: Add CSS for Styling**
Ensure you have CSS classes like `.nav-menu`, `.nav-item`, `.nav-link`, `.nav-icon`, and `.nav-text` to style the navigation bar.

---

### Final Code in :

```javascript
const NAV_ITEMS = [
    { name: "Dashboard", url: "dashboard.html", icon: "dashboard-icon" },
    { name: "Audit Log", url: "audit-log.html", icon: "audit-icon" },
    { name: "Settings", url: "settings.html", icon: "settings-icon" },
];

function renderNavigationMenu() {
    const navBar = document.getElementById("navigation-bar");
    let navHTML = `
        <ul class="nav-menu">
            ${NAV_ITEMS.map(item => `
                <li class="nav-item">
                    <a href="${item.url}" class="nav-link">
                        <span class="nav-icon">${item.icon}</span>
                        <span class="nav-text">${item.name}</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            `).join("")}
        </ul>
    `;
    navBar.innerHTML = navHTML;
}

function setActiveNavItem() {
    const currentPage = window.location.pathname.split("/").pop();
    const navLinks = document.querySelectorAll(".nav-link");

    navLinks.forEach(link => {
        if (link.getAttribute("href") === currentPage) {
            link.classList.add("active");
        } else {
            link.classList.remove("active");
        }
    });
}

document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
    renderNavigationMenu();
    setActiveNavItem();
});
```

This will dynamically create the navigation bar and highlight the active page. You can expand this further to include dropdowns, mobile responsiveness, or other features.

